{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And HaShem spoke to Moses after the death.</b> The word וַיְדַבֵּר, in the sacred tongue, does not for the most part attach to the individual parts of the speech, but to the overall course of the speech as a whole; therefore, after the phrase of speech there comes, for the most part, the phrase of particular utterance. Thus: וַיְדַבֵּר ה׳ אֶל מֹשֶׁה אַחֲרֵי מוֹת... וַיֹּאמֶר אֵלָיו דַּבֵּר אֶל אַהֲרֹן אָחִיךָ (Leviticus 16:1–2). And because he lengthened the account of אַחֲרֵי מוֹת, he did not write וַיֹּאמֶר אֵלָיו immediately, but returned and made mention of HaShem — may He be blessed — as the speaker and Moses as the hearer. However, according to some of our Sages of blessed memory (Torat Kohanim 1:3), there were two acts of speech, and the scriptural reading would be: וַיְדַבֵּר ה׳ אֶל מֹשֶׁה וַיֹּאמֶר אֵלָיו דַּבֵּר אֶל אַהֲרֹן וְאַל יָבֹא וְלֹא יָמוּת. And [the phrase] אַחֲרֵי מוֹת שְׁנֵי בְּנֵי אַהֲרֹן — He said to Moses: דַּבֵּר אֶל אַהֲרֹן וְאַל יָבֹא — that he not enter beyond what he was commanded; וְלֹא יָמוּת — as his sons died when they presumed to offer incense beyond what they were commanded. This serves to urge him more than the first [warning]."], :book :leviticus, :voice :sforno, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T16:36:26.628459569Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}